    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Newton derived Kepler's laws of planetary motion from an inverse square law of force and showed that the law applied across the Solar System, in the moon's motion, the tides, comets and the precession of the equinoxes.</li><li>The book began as a nine-page tract sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmond-halley">Edmond Halley</a> in 1684, grew over about two years of concentrated work, and was published in 1687 at Halley's own expense.</li><li>Its mathematics was accepted readily, but a force acting at a distance drew criticism from Huygens and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottfried-wilhelm-leibniz">Leibniz</a>, and Newton answered with Hypotheses non fingo.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Between May 1684 and April 1686 Newton's chemical notebooks have no entries at all, though he had long kept dated notes of his experiments, which suggests that he did very little else for well over a year and a half.</p></aside>
    </section>

