    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1660s</span><span class="rf-text">Newton studies colliding bodies and shows, for circular planetary motion, an inverse-square relation of force to distance</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1684</span><span class="rf-text">Newton sends Halley the nine-page tract De motu corporum in gyrum</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1686</span><span class="rf-text">The first book is presented to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a>, Hooke makes a priority claim and Pepys gives his imprimatur</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1687</span><span class="rf-text">The complete Principia is published by Halley</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1713</span><span class="rf-text">The second edition appears under the editorship of Roger Cotes, with the General Scholium added</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1726</span><span class="rf-text">The third edition is published, with Rule 4 of the Rules of Reasoning</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1729</span><span class="rf-text">Andrew Motte's English translation appears</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2020</span><span class="rf-text">A new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of owners of the first edition identifies 387 copies</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/edmond-halley"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edmond-halley.jpg" alt="Edmond Halley" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/edmond-halley">Edmond Halley</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edmund_Halley.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Thomas Murray, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Principia grew out of a conversation in January 1684, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmond-halley">Edmond Halley</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-wren">Christopher Wren</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-hooke">Robert Hooke</a> discussed planetary motion. Hooke claimed to have derived the inverse-square law and all the laws of planetary motion but produced no derivation. Halley could derive the inverse-square law only for the circular case, and resolved to ask Newton. In August 1684, probably, Newton told him that he had made the derivation some time before but could not find the papers.</p><p>Newton's earlier work lay behind this. In the 1660s he studied colliding bodies and showed, for circular planetary motion, that the force had an inverse-square relation with distance from the centre. The work followed Copernicus's heliocentric theory of 1543, Kepler's three laws, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei">Galileo</a>'s work on inertia and uniformly accelerated motion, and Descartes's Principia philosophiae of 1644, which held that bodies act on each other only through contact and led to the idea of an aether. A correspondence with Hooke in 1679 and 1680 and a comet seen in the winter of 1680 to 1681 renewed Newton's interest in astronomy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="content">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Principia opens with definitions and the axioms or laws of motion, and continues in three books. Newton defined mass as the quantity of matter arising from density and magnitude, used it to define the quantity of motion, now called momentum, and treated space and time as absolute, distinguishing them from relative measures taken from perceptible objects. Book 1, subtitled De motu corporum, treats motion without a resisting medium. It begins with lemmas on the method of first and last ratios, a geometrical form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calculus">infinitesimal calculus</a>, links centripetal forces to the law of areas and to orbits of conic-section form, and includes the shell theorem and a first study of the three-body problem.</p><p>Book 2 concerns motion through resisting media and makes Newton a pioneer of fluid mechanics. Book 3, De mundi systemate, applies the earlier results to the Solar System. It shows stepwise, beginning with the satellites of Jupiter, that the inverse-square law of mutual gravitation holds universally. It treats the irregularities of the moon's motion, comets, the tides and the precession of the equinoxes, and gives estimates of the relative masses of the giant planets, the Earth and the Sun.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Philosophiæ Naturalis Principia Mathematica&#8221; cites where it covers Book 1, De motu corporum, Book 2, part 2 of De motu corporum, Book 3, De mundi systemate:</p><ul><li>Charles Coulston Gillispie, The Edge of Objectivity: An Essay in the History of Scientific Ideas (Princeton University Press, 1960)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="making">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Was Made</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Newton sent Halley a nine-page <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>, De motu corporum in gyrum, in November 1684. It derived Kepler's three laws from an inverse-square force and generalised the result to conic sections. Halley was so excited by it that he went to Newton again in November 1684 to ask for more, and Newton worked in concentrated fashion until at least mid-1686. His secretary Humphrey Newton later recalled that he sometimes forgot his food or sleep, and that he would rush back from his garden to write down a new thought.</p><p>Newton first conceived the work as two volumes and wrote a fair copy of a second volume, De motu corporum, Liber Secundus, in about the summer of 1685. He then started afresh in a tighter and less accessible mathematical style, which became Book 3. By his own account he had composed it first in a popular method, and recast it in propositions to prevent disputes with readers who could not lay aside their prejudices. Book 1 had meanwhile grown so much that he divided it in two, which is why the book on the system of the world is Book 3.</p><p>Book 1 was presented to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> at the end of April 1686. Hooke's priority claim led Newton, who hated disputes, to threaten to withdraw Book 3, and Halley tactfully persuaded him not to. Halley bore the cost of publication, and the book appeared in 1687.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="reception">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Reception</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-locke"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-locke.jpg" alt="John Locke" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/john-locke">John Locke</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Godfrey_Kneller_-_Portrait_of_John_Locke_(Hermitage).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Godfrey Kneller, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The mathematical parts of the first two books were so clearly consistent that they were easily accepted. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-locke">John Locke</a> asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christiaan-huygens">Christiaan Huygens</a> whether he could trust the proofs and was assured of their correctness. The idea of an attractive force acting at a distance received a cooler response. Huygens and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottfried-wilhelm-leibniz">Leibniz</a> noted that the law was incompatible with the notion of an aether, and from a Cartesian point of view it was a faulty theory. Newton replied that the mathematical form of the theory had to be correct because it explained the data, and he refused to speculate on the nature of gravity. The number of phenomena that the theory organised impressed younger philosophers, who soon adopted the methods and language of the Principia.</p><p>In response to criticism that he had introduced occult agencies, Newton added the General Scholium to the second edition of 1713 and wrote there Hypotheses non fingo, that he formulated no hypotheses. In the same text he criticised Descartes's vortex theory and inferred the existence of a god from the system of the world. It has been suggested that this gave an oblique argument for a unitarian conception of God. Later writers praised the book. Clairaut wrote in 1747 that it marked the epoch of a great revolution in physics, and Lagrange called it the greatest production of the human mind.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="survival">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Survival &amp; Rediscovery</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gottfried-wilhelm-leibniz"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gottfried-wilhelm-leibniz.jpg" alt="Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gottfried-wilhelm-leibniz">Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Christoph_Bernhard_Francke_-_Bildnis_des_Philosophen_Leibniz_(ca._1695).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Christoph Bernhard Francke, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The first edition was probably printed in about 500 copies, though estimates have been higher. A survey published in 1953 located 189 surviving copies, and a 2020 study counted 387 owners' copies. It suggests a wider initial readership and a greater early influence on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> science than had been assumed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> University Library holds Newton's own copy of the first edition, interleaved with blank pages for his corrections. The Huntington Library has another of his copies, with annotations in his hand. The Bodmer Library in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> keeps a copy once owned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottfried-wilhelm-leibniz">Leibniz</a>, with his notes on the dispute over who first formulated calculus. A first edition at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppsala-university">Uppsala University</a> Library was stolen in the 1960s and returned in 2009.</p><p>Newton had been urged since the early 1690s to prepare a new edition because copies had grown rare and costly. Richard Bentley arranged the second edition and appointed Roger Cotes as editor, who announced its publication on 30 June 1713. Newton gave no acknowledgement to Cotes, and omitted others, John Flamsteed above all, because of priority disputes. The third edition was published on 25 March 1726 under Henry Pemberton, and the second edition was the basis of the first edition printed abroad, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> in 1714.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/emilie-du-chatelet"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emilie-du-chatelet.jpg" alt="Émilie du Châtelet" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/emilie-du-chatelet">Émilie du Châtelet</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emilie_Chatelet_portrait_by_Latour.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Maurice Quentin de La Tour, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Four full English translations have been made from the third edition. The first, by Andrew Motte in 1729, was the basis of a modernised version of 1934 under Florian Cajori's name. I. Bernard Cohen, Anne Whitman and Julia Budenz published a new translation in 1999, after Cohen criticised the 1934 version. Charles Leedham-Green published another with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> University Press in 2021. In 1739 to 1742 two Minim priests, Thomas LeSeur and François Jacquier, produced an annotated edition, often called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> edition, which was reprinted more than once in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> in the 19th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emilie-du-chatelet">Émilie du Châtelet</a> translated all three books into French, with a commentary that applied calculus to Newton's most controversial theories, and hers remains the standard French translation.</p><p>The sequence of definitions in the Principia is recognisable in many textbooks today, and its four Rules of Reasoning in Philosophy have been described as the basis of modern approaches to science. In 1977 the Voyager spacecraft carried a picture of a page of the Principia on the Golden Record, and in 2014 the astronaut Tim Peake named his mission to the International Space Station Principia after the book.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Philosophiæ Naturalis Principia Mathematica&#8221; cites where it covers Legacy:</p><ul><li>James R. Newman, The World of Mathematics (George Allen &amp; Unwin, 1956)</li><li>George Finlay Simmons, Calculus Gems: Brief Lives and Memorable Mathematics (American Mathematical Society, 2019)</li></ul></details>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Principia is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-newton">Isaac Newton</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> work of 1687 on the mathematical principles of natural philosophy, setting out his laws of motion and law of universal gravitation. Prompted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmond-halley">Edmond Halley</a> and published at his expense, it was revised in 1713 and 1726 and translated many times.</em></p>

